Subject: faster JSON.parse and JSON.stringify on the paths apps use

What this does
  Speeds up JSON on the shapes an app actually serializes and deserializes:
  integer-heavy arrays, plain ASCII strings, and small nested objects.

What it changes
  - JSON.stringify: cycle detection moves from a JSValue array to a plain
    heap stack of borrowed object pointers, so push/pop no longer allocate
    or hit the GC. A per-stringify check also learns whether
    Object.prototype or Array.prototype carries a toJSON method, letting
    plain objects and arrays skip the per-node toJSON lookup entirely.
    Integers are formatted straight into the output buffer instead of
    building a temporary JSString.
  - JS_ToQuotedString: a 256-entry escape lookup table plus a bulk copy of
    the runs that need no escaping, replacing the per-character loop.
  - JSON.parse: a pure-ASCII string with no escapes is built directly from
    the source range. Integer literals that fit in a double are read
    without a strtod call. Array elements are staged in a C buffer and a
    dense array is built in one shot, avoiding a property define per
    element, when there is no reviver.

Why we need it
  JSON is the serialization format React Native uses for the bridge and
  native bindings, so its hot shapes dominate real payloads. The engine's
  parser and serializer allocated per element and per character on paths
  these fast paths make constant-time.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No. This changes only runtime behaviour inside quickjs.c; the bytecode
  format is untouched.

Notes
  Ported from the research tree (0001-json-fast-paths) and re-derived against
  this tree, which already had upstream's batch-ASCII string parse path. The
  numbers quoted in the source header were measured against a different
  engine commit and a different collector, so they are not carried over here;
  the effect is re-measured by tools/benchmark.

  The JSON.stringify cycle-stack holds borrowed object pointers that are all
  alive on the recursion stack, so it needs no refcounting. json_parse_value
  hands the staged element buffer to JS_NewArrayFrom, which takes ownership
  of the individual values on error; the buffer itself is freed here.
